
    // ── LazyVerilog patch: reader-thread message preview ──────────────────
    //
    /// Observe every incoming message as soon as it is framed, on the thread
    /// that drains the input stream, before it is queued for dispatch.
    ///
    /// LspCpp reads messages on one thread and asio::post()s each of them to a
    /// thread pool, so a handler only ever sees messages that have already been
    /// dispatched.  From inside a handler there is no way to learn that a newer
    /// message for the same document is already in flight: the pending work
    /// sits in asio's own task queue, which exposes no introspection, and each
    /// entry is still an unparsed string.  This callback is the one place where
    /// that is visible.
    ///
    /// It runs on the reader thread, so it must be cheap and must never block —
    /// anything it waits on stops the server reading its input.  It receives the
    /// raw message and takes no position on what that means; the policy belongs
    /// to the server.
    ///
    /// Set it before startProcessingMessages(); it is not synchronized.
    void setIncomingMessagePreview(std::function<void(std::string const&)> preview)
    {
        incoming_message_preview_ = std::move(preview);
    }

private:
    std::function<void(std::string const&)> incoming_message_preview_;

public:
    // ── end LazyVerilog patch ─────────────────────────────────────────────
